Core Feature Takeaways for relative to our MVP
Real-Time Sync Implement CalDAV/WebDAV or provider-specific REST APIs (Google, Microsoft, Apple) with bi-directional syncing and push notifications.

Natural-Language Parsing Use libraries like parsedatetime or Duckling for “next Tuesday at 3pm” style event creation.

Cross-Platform UI – Web: React/Angular/Vue calendar‐UI (e.g., FullCalendar) – Desktop: Electron or native wrappers – Mobile: Flutter or React Native

Authentication & Security – OAuth2 for 3rd-party calendar access – JWT for session management – Role-based access controls for shared calendars

Scheduling Logic – Free/busy matching – Time-zone calculations via moment-timezone – Buffer times & recurrence rules using RFC 5545 (iCal)

Integrations & Extensibility – Webhooks for external triggers – Plugin architecture for video-conferencing add-ons (Zoom, Teams, Meet) – Zapier and Integromat endpoints for no-code workflows

By analyzing these market leaders—downloads, pricing tiers, and technical capabilities—you can distill the must-have features for your MVP. Focus first on robust bi-directional syncing, natural-language event creation, and a modular authentication/integration layer. Once your core calendar engine is rock-solid, you can layer on AI-driven scheduling, analytics, and advanced collaboration features to truly stand out.
